"""Renders a ``PIL.Image.Image`` in the terminal via Kitty's own Terminal
Graphics Protocol (https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/graphics-protocol/),
deliberately with no other protocol and no third-party terminal-graphics
library involved -- Ghostty (this app's actual target terminal) speaks
Kitty's protocol directly, so there's nothing an auto-detecting multi-
protocol library adds here except more code paths to go wrong in.

Technique: transmit the image once (chunked base64 PNG), then instead of
an absolutely-positioned overlay (which doesn't compose with a normal
character-grid renderer, and breaks under scrolling), draw a placement
using Kitty's "Unicode placeholder" trick -- a private-use codepoint
followed by row/column diacritics, styled with a foreground color that
encodes the image id. Kitty recognizes this exact pattern and paints the
image there instead of the literal glyphs. This is what lets image output
flow through Rich/Textual's ordinary Segment-based rendering (and scroll
correctly inside ``VerticalScroll`` along with everything else) rather
than needing a separate absolute-positioning/compositing system.

Sizing: the image is transmitted at its original resolution (downscaled
only if huge, purely to bound upload size -- see ``_MAX_TRANSMIT_SIDE``)
and the placement is given a target column/row count; Kitty itself scales
to fit that box terminal-side. This avoids needing this module to query
the terminal's own cell pixel size (an ioctl call with no real risk, but
one more thing to get right) just to pre-scale client-side -- letting the
already-battle-tested terminal-side scaler do that job instead.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

